\paragraph{Cross-domain convergent nocuity.} Among the 50 ambiguous healthcare clauses, 58.0\% trigger majority-convergent violations ($\geq$3/5 models; 34.0\% unanimous), compared to 41.3\% and 13.0\% respectively in the main $\tau^2$-bench experiment. The permissive attractor is near-universal: 100\% of convergent violations reflect permissive interpretation (vs.\ 77\% in the main experiment). Cross-family pairwise Jaccard (mean $= 0.69$, $n = 9$) exceeds within-family Jaccard ($0.56$, $n = 1$)---a reversal of the main-experiment pattern (cross $0.46$ $<$ within $0.54$). This reversal is consistent with a stronger permissive attractor in healthcare: when ambiguity violations are nearly universal and uniformly permissive, model-family differences are overwhelmed, and cross-family convergence approaches or exceeds within-family levels. These results provide pilot-scale cross-domain evidence ($n = 50$) that convergent nocuity generalizes beyond customer service and intensifies in high-stakes domains.

\section{Cross-Judge Validation}
\label{app:cross_judge}

To assess whether violation judgments depend on the specific LLM judge, we re-evaluate all 2,997 ambiguous-condition episodes using three independent judge families: GPT-4.1 (original), Claude Sonnet~4.6, and Gemini~2.5~Pro. Each judge uses the same rubric and prompt template; only the underlying model differs.

\paragraph{Inter-judge agreement.} Fleiss' $\kappa$ across all three judges is 0.717 (substantial agreement). Pairwise Cohen's $\kappa$: GPT--Claude $= 0.776$, GPT--Gemini $= 0.696$, Claude--Gemini $= 0.685$. Gemini is systematically harsher (mean $+$8.8\,pp across all models), while GPT and Claude produce nearly identical rates (mean $|\Delta| = 1.6$\,pp).

\paragraph{Model ranking concordance.} All three judges agree on ranking extremes: Claude Sonnet~4.6 is least vulnerable (rank~5 in all three) and DeepSeek-V3 ranks 1--2 in all three. Pairwise Spearman $\rho$ for model rankings: GPT--Claude $= 0.70$, GPT--Gemini $= 0.80$, Claude--Gemini $= 0.70$ (all $p < 0.20$; with only five models, power is inherently limited).

\paragraph{Type ranking concordance.} Pairwise Spearman $\rho$ for type rankings: GPT--Claude $= 0.71$ ($p = 0.11$), GPT--Gemini $= 0.77$ ($p = 0.07$), Claude--Gemini $= 0.89$ ($p = 0.019$). All three judges rank incompleteness highest ($\geq$46.8\%) and coreferential or conditional precedence lowest. Authorization scope shows the largest cross-judge variance (41.6\%--65.6\%), consistent with its low per-type $\kappa$ (0.385; Table~\ref{tab:per_type_agreement}).

\paragraph{Same-family bias.} No same-family leniency is observed. The Claude judge rates Claude Sonnet~4.6's authorization scope violations at 30\%---\emph{higher} than the GPT judge's 12\%---indicating stricter, not more lenient, evaluation of same-family outputs. Similarly, the GPT judge rates GPT-5.4 at 64\% (the highest of any model), showing no ingroup leniency.

\subsection{Models}

We evaluate five models from four providers spanning two architecture families: GPT-5.4 and GPT-4.1 (OpenAI, proprietary dense), Claude Sonnet 4.6 (Anthropic, proprietary dense), Qwen3-235B (Alibaba, open-source Mixture-of-Experts), and DeepSeek-V3 (DeepSeek, open-source dense). This selection covers frontier and mid-tier proprietary models alongside open-source alternatives with different training philosophies. All API calls use temperature $= 0$, top\_p $= 1$, and max\_tokens $= 4096$, and were made on May 20, 2026.

\subsection{Evaluation}

We use a cross-judge design to mitigate self-evaluation bias: GPT-5.4 judges Claude, GPT-4.1, and Qwen3 traces; GPT-4.1 judges GPT-5.4 and DeepSeek traces. Because our matched-pair design compares conditions within the same model--judge pairing, systematic judge bias cancels in the treatment effect ($\Delta$pp); cross-model judge asymmetry affects only absolute rates, not C1 or C2 (\S\ref{sec:limitations}). Judges classify episodes on a four-tier severity scale (none, minor, moderate, critical); our primary threshold is moderate-or-above (Appendix~\ref{app:severity}). Dual-judge calibration on 100 stratified episodes yields $\kappa = 0.653$ ($\kappa_w = 0.775$; per-type $n \approx 17$, CIs wide; Appendix~\ref{app:per_type_agreement}). Three-family validation (GPT-5.4, GPT-4.1, Claude; Appendix~\ref{app:cross_judge}) yields Fleiss' $\kappa = 0.717$. The judge prompt template is in Appendix~\ref{app:judge_prompt}.

